#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tunnel-fw - nftables builder for two-hop tunnel setups:

    clients ──client tunnel──▶ FE (ingress) ══transport tunnel══▶ GW (gateway)

  client tunnel     what the clients build to the gateway; it rides inside the transport tunnel
  transport tunnel  the FE <-> gateway link that carries client traffic
                    on an FE: upstream = toward the gateway, downstream = toward the clients
                    (only an FE in the middle of a chain has a downstream tunnel)

Each tunnel can be WireGuard, OpenVPN or an SSH VPN (ssh -w), in any combination
(e.g. WireGuard over OpenVPN, WireGuard over SSH, SSH over SSH),
and either end of a transport tunnel can be the listening side. One gateway can be
fed by several FEs, each with its own transport tunnel, and can host several client
tunnels (e.g. one per FE leg).

Run it on each server. It asks what role the box plays and which ports/services
to keep open, then writes /etc/nftables.conf, applies it with an automatic
rollback guard, enables it at boot, and clears stale NAT conntrack state.

Commands:
  setup      interactive wizard (default)      tunnel-fw setup [--dry-run]
  apply      re-apply from saved config         tunnel-fw apply [--yes]
  render     print the ruleset for the config   tunnel-fw render
  doctor     health-check the tunnel path       tunnel-fw doctor [--fix]
  edit       change/add/delete from a menu      tunnel-fw edit

Multiple FEs feeding one gateway (run on the gateway):
  add-fe     add another FE's transport tunnel  tunnel-fw add-fe [--name fe2 --dev fe2 ...]
  remove-fe  drop an FE's transport tunnel      tunnel-fw remove-fe fe2
  list-fe    show transports + active clients   tunnel-fw list-fe
  join-nets  let clients on every FE's subnet   tunnel-fw join-nets 172.23.1.0/24,172.23.2.0/24
             reach each other (--off to undo)

Stdlib only; needs root for everything except `render` and `--dry-run`.
"""

